**Merge sort** (divide and conquer, O(n log n) time in all cases, O(n) extra space, stable):

```java
static void sort(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {   // hi exclusive
    if (hi - lo < 2) return;
    int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
    sort(a, lo, mid);
    sort(a, mid, hi);
    int[] tmp = new int[hi - lo];
    int i = lo, j = mid, k = 0;
    while (i < mid && j < hi) tmp[k++] = a[i] <= a[j] ? a[i++] : a[j++];
    while (i < mid) tmp[k++] = a[i++];
    while (j < hi)  tmp[k++] = a[j++];
    System.arraycopy(tmp, 0, a, lo, tmp.length);
}
```

Using `<=` keeps equal elements in order (stability). Recursion depth is O(log n).

**Deadlock** needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Two threads take two locks in opposite order:

```java
Object A = new Object(), B = new Object();
Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
    synchronized (A) { sleep(100); synchronized (B) { } }
});
Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
    synchronized (B) { sleep(100); synchronized (A) { } }
});
t1.start(); t2.start();
```

(`sleep` is a small helper that wraps `Thread.sleep`.) The short sleep makes it very likely that each thread holds its first lock before trying for the second, so both block forever.

**How to explain it.** t1 holds A and waits for B; t2 holds B and waits for A: a circular wait.

**Detecting and fixing.** Take a thread dump (`jstack`) and the JVM reports the deadlock. Prevention: always acquire locks in a fixed global order; use `tryLock(timeout)` from `ReentrantLock` and back off; reduce lock scope; or use higher-level concurrent structures. Mention that livelock and starvation are different problems.

**Tip.** State complexity and edge cases for merge sort (empty array, one element, duplicates) and say that you can also do it bottom-up to avoid recursion.
